**Read:** [A Review of Operations Research in Mine Planning — Newman and colleagues](../sources/Operations-Research-in-Mine-Planning.lingua#r_3WX1KW34EWEGYZ6HGEMGGQBKHS), the complete review and selected original formulation paper; [MineLib — Espinoza, Goycoolea, Moreno, and Newman](../sources/MineLib-Benchmarks-and-Methods.lingua#r_3KXVJNVZ2FSQ8CZJMJG2JDQYR8), the complete paper and exact selected problem specification; [Surface Mining Technology — Kaushik Dey, NPTEL](../sources/Surface-Mining-Technology-NPTEL.lingua#r_5J36XSV6KZRNWX3J51Q4TRV7KG), the full phases/sequences context.

A block or activity precedence describes an identified dependency: access, overlying material, support/backfill, ventilation, development, or another condition. Its physical meaning, time convention, and uncertainty belong to the model. A precedence graph can simplify geometry while hiding working-space or sequence requirements.

An ultimate-pit model chooses an admissible set under value and geospatial dependencies. A schedule additionally assigns time and capacities; a detailed operating design adds geometry, equipment, utilities, people, and actual conditions. The same block values can therefore produce different outputs for different problems.

Define whether predecessors must be completed in an earlier period or may share a period under further sequencing. Define unselected material, destinations, processing, stockpiles, and the scope of capacity. Omitting a dependency can yield an attractive objective that cannot be physically reached.

**Work:** construct a small original dependency graph from a stated excavation model and explain each arc. Compare a set-only solution with a time/capacity account and identify a missing operational constraint. Read a complete MineLib problem definition and map its quantities to the model, preserving what it intentionally abstracts.

Read the full operations-research review and selected primary methods. Benchmark feasibility and optimality must be checked against the exact stated formulation rather than inferred from a solver's successful termination or a three-dimensional display.

